WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.947
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.947 --> 00:00:10.120
The day I got an offer to be
a consultant after college,

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.080
a good friend told me, girl, you're done.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.110
The hardest part is getting the job.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.970
That got me really excited.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.800
But I also heard this anxious
voice speaking inside my head.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.131
That voice is my inner
critic who I call Nancy.

00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:32.693
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And Nancy kept telling me,

00:00:32.693 --> 00:00:37.160
[SOUND] Renata,
I don't think you should be that excited.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.715
You have zero consulting skills.

00:00:39.715 --> 00:00:42.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Nancy was right.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.280
But she calmed down after
all of the training

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.000
that every consultant has to get through.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.010
A few years later,
when I met my wonderful partner,

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.200
John, I had a strangely
similar experience.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.810
Although my friends tell me, you're done,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.105
the hardest part is
finding the right person.

00:01:00.105 --> 00:01:03.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I was really excited.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.920
But Nancy came back screaming.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.710
Renata, what do you know
about relationships?

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.760
Clearly not much because so
far 100% of them have ended.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.050
Ouch
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.370
&gt;&gt; I know.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.560
&gt;&gt; But before I tell you what happened
next, I want to take a quick poll.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:28.630
Raise your hand if you think that love
is a pretty important part of life.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.890
Everyone, all of us, great.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.640
Now, raise your hand if you feel
like you've been really well

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.328
educated on how to love.

00:01:39.328 --> 00:01:42.205
None of us.

00:01:42.205 --> 00:01:44.018
Let's think about that for a second.

00:01:44.018 --> 00:01:46.610
We all value our carriers.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.090
And if you're relatively well educated on
how to succeed, that's how we got here.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:54.810
But when it comes to love,
which overwhelming research

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.220
points as a top predictor of
life satisfaction, health and

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:03.740
wealth, we kind of just wait or
swipe until we find the right person.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.310
We look at the couples around us,
we watch what they do right and

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.860
wrong, and
we assume we know how to do right.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.918
But that's like hoping to become
a doctor by watching Grey's Anatomy.

00:02:15.918 --> 00:02:18.033
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:20.043
that's what I was thinking
about when I met John.

00:02:20.043 --> 00:02:23.430
I felt like I finally
met the right person.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.270
But I didn't feel educated on
how to be the right person.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:32.550
And I started getting really scared that
I would eventually mess everything up.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.240
And I really didn't want
to mess anything up.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.660
So I started reading every
piece of research, and

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.420
every book that I could find, about
the science of love and relationships.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.740
Luckily, John loves reading, so
he followed me down that rabbit hole.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.023
Which is very convenient but
also a little weird.

00:02:52.023 --> 00:02:56.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now we talk about our relationship and

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.930
feelings in terms of stuff
like attachment theory.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:02.970
And the neurobiology of desire and
emotions.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.829
And we haven't even taken.

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:08.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Even though it might not be sexy to

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.890
talk about your relationship in scientific
terms, it's kind of like having food

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.910
in your teeth, you really want to know so
you can do something about it.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.491
I have to say though, I try to be really
discreet about my interest in relationship

00:03:22.491 --> 00:03:23.871
topics for a long time.

00:03:23.871 --> 00:03:26.065
I felt it was super controversial and

00:03:26.065 --> 00:03:29.900
frankly too embarrassing to
discuss with anyone else but John.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:35.510
But then one day, my brother,
Rodrigo, called me to ask.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.540
Hey, is everything okay between you and
John?

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.230
And I said, yeah, what's going on?

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.250
Well, my brother and

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:50.100
I happen to share an Amazon account,
which sometimes gets awkward.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.800
He was calling because he noticed
the trend in my recent book orders.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.130
Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.395
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Your Brain on Love,

00:04:02.395 --> 00:04:04.545
Mating in Captivity.

00:04:04.545 --> 00:04:06.357
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And many,

00:04:06.357 --> 00:04:11.380
many other relationship books
that John and I read together.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.645
We don't do Netflix and
chill, we read and chill.

00:04:13.645 --> 00:04:17.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now when my brother called me,

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.920
he was also getting an MBA.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.600
So I told him, listen, we're both getting
an MBA because it's valuable, but

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.440
it's not always been valuable.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.250
When the MBA was created 100 years ago,
people thought it was crazy that business

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:35.220
was being elevated to the same level
of expertise as law and engineering.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.820
Now it's the most popular
degree in the US.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.960
We finally see value in
business education, and

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.540
we have to do the same for love.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.030
Now am I suggesting you create
a formal master's degree in love?

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:51.260
No, but

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:57.120
I am suggesting we all start seeing
ourselves as CEOs of our own love lives.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.110
We know from research,
that couples who thrive and

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.150
couples who fail behave very differently.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.898
We also know from research
that just like in business,

00:05:08.898 --> 00:05:13.180
there's no formula, but there are tools.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.940
And just like great CEOs,

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.310
couples that thrive master tools
that make them better everyday.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:26.548
Today, I'm going to share my top three
favorites, like a good consultant.

00:05:26.548 --> 00:05:28.538
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Number one,

00:05:28.538 --> 00:05:31.480
choose to see love as a skill.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.740
By far the most important thing
that I've learned from this journey

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.330
is that love is not something
that happens to us.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:39.500
It's a skill.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:42.760
We can choose to learn,
practice, and master over time.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.030
It's not easy, and
we'll never be perfect, but

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.400
seeing it as a skill is the first
step to getting better.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.290
Number two,
choose generosity over judgment.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.320
If you've been the parent of a child or
a puppy, you know what it feels like

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.030
to see really unpleasant behavior
with generosity, not judgment.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.460
You understand that
they're still growing up.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:11.476
But if our partner does something we don't
like, [SOUND] we judge we get offended.

00:06:11.476 --> 00:06:12.950
They should be fully grown up by now.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:17.950
But as long as we are live,
we're all still growing up.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.780
It's easy to love the wonderful about
our partner, but the true love skill

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.877
is seeing what's not so wonderful about
them with generosity, not judgment.

00:06:29.877 --> 00:06:34.490
Number three, choose to become
an expert on your partner.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:41.950
We know from neuroscience that if you take
couples who report to still be madly in

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:47.480
love after 25 years and put them through
an MRI machine, when you show them a photo

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:52.320
of their partner, their brain lights up
just like couples who just fell in love.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:58.252
Which is crazy because that basically
means that the honeymoon phase

00:06:58.252 --> 00:07:01.154
doesn't have to be over at the honeymoon.

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:06.630
Couples who stay in love
are couples who stay curious.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:10.290
Every day they commit to learning
about each other like an expert.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:11.420
They never stop learning.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.230
It's a bit ironic that we find
a romantic to say things like,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.890
my god,
I know everything about my boyfriend.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:26.790
Personally, I find it much more romantic
to think about how much more I want to

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:30.530
know about my partner and how much more
of an expert I want to become everyday.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:38.370
Now these tools are not a checklist,
they are a lifelong practice.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.570
But it's easy to forget that
when we get busy with life.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.110
So I want to leave you with something that

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.799
remind myself of being accountable
every day both in business and in life.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:58.210
As CEOs in love, or business, we're all
given two options every single day.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:05.870
You can either blame external conditions
for your performance, or you can

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:09.380
take radical responsibility for practicing
the skills that make you better.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:14.222
Now the question is,
which one will you choose?

00:08:14.222 --> 00:08:16.146
Thank you.

00:08:16.146 --> 00:08:17.693
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:17.693 --> 00:08:22.502
[MUSIC]

